{0 carddav}

CardDAV for OCaml. An implementation of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6352.html}RFC 6352} on {!Httpz_dav} and
{!Fetch_dav}, with the sync-collection report of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6578.html}RFC 6578} and the discovery of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6764.html}RFC 6764}.

{1 Three libraries}

- [carddav] is the protocol. {!Carddav.Property} names the address book
  properties, {!Carddav.Filter} and {!Carddav.Report} build the
  addressbook-query and addressbook-multiget reports and read their answers,
  {!Carddav.Addressbook} reads an address book collection from a PROPFIND,
  {!Carddav.Error} names the conditions, and {!Carddav.Data} is the
  representation of an address object a client reads and writes.
- [carddav.eio] is the client. {!Carddav_eio.Client.connect} finds the principal
  and its address book home from a URL, and every other function is one request
  over {!Fetch_dav}.
- [carddav.jscontact] is {!Carddav_jscontact.card}, the representation that
  reads and writes address objects as JSContact cards through the conversion of
  {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9555.html}RFC 9555}.

{1 One address book, three views}

A CardDAV server stores vCards. Every client function that reads or writes an
address object takes a {!Carddav.Data.t}, and the same call yields the vCard
text with {!Carddav.Data.raw}, a {!Vcard.t} with {!Carddav.Data.vcard} or a
{!Jscontact.Card.t} with {!Carddav_jscontact.card}:

{[
Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
let credentials = [ Fetch.Credential.basic ~user:"alice" ~password:"secret" ] in
let client =
  Carddav_eio.Client.connect ~sw ~credentials (Fetch_httpz.std env)
    "https://contacts.example.com/.well-known/carddav"
  |> Result.get_ok
in
let books = Carddav_eio.Client.addressbooks client |> Result.get_ok in
let book = (List.hd books).href in
let cards, _ =
  Carddav_eio.Client.query Carddav_jscontact.card client book
    Carddav.Filter.(v [ prop "EMAIL" [ text_match "example.com" ] ])
  |> Result.get_ok
in
List.iter (fun (e : _ Carddav_eio.Client.entry) -> print_endline e.value.uid) cards
]}

A card read this way carries the vCard properties, parameters and names that
have no JSContact counterpart, so writing it back with
{!Carddav_eio.Client.put} loses nothing.

{1 Synchronisation}

{!Carddav_eio.Client.val-sync} runs the sync-collection report and fetches the
changed members by multiget, so a client keeps one token per address book and
receives the members that were added, changed or removed since. A server that
forgets a token answers with the [DAV:valid-sync-token] condition, and the
client starts again without one.

{1 Servers that differ}

A profile in {!Carddav_eio.Quirks} names what one service does differently, and
the client consults it only where the difference matters.
{!Carddav_eio.Quirks.fastmail} is applied to any Fastmail host by default, and
{!Carddav_eio.Quirks.standard} expects the RFC.

{1 Testing against a server}

The unit tests check the library against the examples of the RFCs. The tests in
[test/carddav/oracle] run against a Radicale server in docker, started by
[scripts/carddav-up.sh], and are skipped unless [CARDDAV_ORACLE_URL] is set.
They are the evidence for the profiles of {!Carddav_eio.Quirks}.
